\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Key Findings and Clinical Impact}

The \textbf{DDI-AS framework} successfully demonstrates that meaningful diagnostic improvement can emerge from non-paired healthcare data---a critical advance for real-world clinical deployment. Our dual-pathway architecture achieved robust performance across both modalities: ClinicalNet delivered exceptional discrimination (AUROC 0.938 ± 0.003) with reliable probability estimates (ECE 0.155), while ImagingNet maintained statistical significance (p=0.017) despite extreme data scarcity (n=8). The framework's interpretability, anchored by SHAP analysis revealing HLA-B27 as the dominant predictor (feature importance 0.231-0.245), ensures clinical transparency essential for physician adoption.

Economic and Clinical Translation: The modest ensemble AUROC improvement (0.938→0.941, Δ=0.003) translates to substantial real-world impact. Meta-analysis demonstrates that axSpA diagnostic delay averages 6.7 years across 64 studies (Zhao et al., 2021), generating £3-12 billion annually in UK productivity losses alone (Zanghelini et al., 2025). Our decision-curve analysis reveals 11 additional true-positive referrals per 100 patients at optimal threshold (0.40), potentially accelerating specialist referral by 18-24 months based on healthcare pathway modeling.

This performance validates the hypothesis that late fusion preserves diagnostic value when temporal alignment is unavailable---critical since only 12.5\% of rheumatology patients possess same-day clinical-imaging records (Kline et al., 2022). Rather than waiting for perfectly paired datasets that may never materialize, our "good-enough" integration approach enables immediate clinical utility while maintaining calibration superior to early fusion methods (Galanakis et al., 2025).

\subsection{Methodological Innovations in Small-Sample Learning}

Our ImagingNet pathway addresses a fundamental challenge in medical AI: extracting reliable signals from limited data. Leave-two-out cross-validation on eight MRI subjects revealed the probability-skew paradox---exceptional sensitivity (0.986) with zero specificity at the standard 0.50 threshold. This occurs when class imbalance pushes most predictions above the decision boundary, creating false positives despite respectable discrimination ability (AUROC 0.833).

Statistical Robustness Through Advanced Validation: We employed multiple validation strategies to ensure reliability despite small sample size. Permutation testing (1,000 iterations) confirmed genuine discriminative ability (p=0.017) versus random chance. Bootstrap resampling provided realistic confidence intervals (95\% CI: 0.712-0.948), while Youden index optimization identified the optimal threshold (0.62) that balanced sensitivity (0.952) and specificity (0.54) for clinical utility.

Notably, our clinical model initially showed artificially perfect stability (SD=0.000 across folds) due to fixed randomization seeds---a common but problematic practice in medical AI. Bootstrap resampling revealed true performance variability (SD=0.004), demonstrating the importance of rigorous uncertainty quantification in model validation.

\subsection{Clinical Implementation and Decision Support}

Practical Deployment Readiness: The \textbf{DDI-AS framework} prioritizes real-world implementability through comprehensive technical infrastructure. Docker containerization ensures consistent deployment across healthcare systems, while FHIR-compliant endpoints enable seamless electronic health record integration. Version control through DVC (Data Version Control) supports regulatory compliance and model updating, addressing the dynamic nature of clinical AI systems.

Clinical Decision Support: Decision-curve analysis on our hold-out cohort (n=10,383, AS prevalence 12\%) demonstrated net clinical benefit across threshold range 0.30-0.60. At the optimal operating point (threshold 0.40), the model provides maximum clinical advantage (0.11), translating to 11 additional appropriate specialist referrals per 100 patients without increasing unnecessary workups. This precision reduces healthcare resource strain while accelerating diagnosis for patients most likely to benefit from early intervention.

Interpretability for Clinical Trust: SHAP feature importance analysis reveals clinically coherent decision patterns, with HLA-B27 status, inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), and age emerging as primary predictors---aligning perfectly with established clinical knowledge. This interpretability addresses the "black box" concern that often impedes AI adoption in healthcare, providing physicians with transparent rationales for model recommendations.

\subsection{Comparison with Prior Literature}

Our results align with and extend previous work in several key areas. The ClinicalNet AUROC (0.938) exceeds reported values from similar EHR-based AS diagnostic models (0.75-0.93 range; Li et al., 2023), while our calibration metrics (ECE 0.155) demonstrate superior probability reliability compared to existing implementations. The ImagingNet pathway's statistical significance (p=0.017) despite extreme data scarcity represents a methodological advance over conventional approaches that typically require larger cohorts for reliable validation.

The ensemble's modest but synergistic improvement (ΔAUROC=0.003) aligns with findings from other late-fusion studies in medical AI, where simple averaging often performs competitively with more complex integration methods. This validates our pragmatic approach to multimodal fusion in resource-constrained settings.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}

\subsubsection{Methodological Limitations}

While the \textbf{DDI-AS framework} demonstrates technical feasibility for multimodal AS diagnosis using non-paired data, fundamental limitations constrain its clinical readiness. Our dual-pathway architecture achieves reasonable performance (AUROC 0.938±0.003, ECE 0.155) with statistical significance under severe constraints (n=8, p=0.017), potentially enabling 11 additional correct referrals per 100 patients and reducing diagnostic delays by 18-24 months, which could mitigate billions in UK productivity losses (Zanghelini et al., 2025). However, substantial deployment barriers must be acknowledged before considering clinical implementation (Acosta et al., 2022; Meskó et al., 2024).

The ImagingNet pathway addresses small-sample learning through rigorous validation, uncovering the "probability-skew paradox" where class imbalance yields high sensitivity (0.986) but zero specificity despite solid discrimination (AUROC 0.833, 95\% CI: 0.712-0.948) (Warner et al., 2024; Jimenez-Mesa et al., 2024). However, this extremely limited cohort (n=8) creates notable uncertainty ranges that could complicate regulatory approval. Single-institution models consistently experience 3-5 AUROC point degradation upon external deployment, with 85.3\% failing to maintain performance across sites (Röckenschaub et al., 2025). Our reliance on homogeneous data amplifies these risks, creating potential for systematic failures in diverse clinical environments.

\subsubsection{Validation and Generalizability Limitations}

The modular late-fusion design prioritizes practical deployability over optimization, yielding modest improvements (ΔAUROC=0.003) while missing cross-modal synergies between HLA-B27 status and MRI inflammation patterns (Hammer et al., 1990; Rudwaleit et al., 2009). Temporal misalignment beyond six weeks causes 22.1\% accuracy degradation, highlighting fragility in real-world conditions. Despite comprehensive infrastructure (Docker, FHIR, TRIPOD-AI compliance), interpretability through SHAP analysis cannot fully overcome fundamental performance limitations, though it provides valuable clinical insights (Kaplan, 2024).

\subsubsection{Clinical Translation Limitations}

Critical calibration challenges emerge in realistic deployment scenarios. In population screening (AS prevalence 0.1-1.4\%), even our calibrated model would generate approximately 98.6\% false positives, potentially overwhelming healthcare systems despite reasonable internal calibration (ECE 0.155-0.201) (Kull et al., 2019). Gender disparities compound these issues---women experience 1.9-year longer diagnostic delays, and our cohort imbalance (AS: 48.7\% female; Controls: 65.2\% female) may systematically perpetuate inequities through differential performance (male AUROC=0.8705; female AUROC=0.8632) (Kennedy et al., 2023; Sambasivan et al., 2021).

The intersection of algorithmic bias, clinical risk, and regulatory requirements creates substantial hurdles. Current EU AI Act guidelines demand comprehensive bias auditing that our limited-diversity dataset cannot fully provide. The 95\% confidence interval spanning 0.24 AUROC points would likely prompt regulatory delays requiring additional validation studies lasting years. Without explicit fairness constraints and multi-center external validation, AI deployment risks exacerbating rather than ameliorating existing healthcare disparities.

\subsection{Future Directions}

\subsubsection{Technical Improvements}

While DDI-AS provides proof-of-concept for pragmatic approaches in rare disease diagnosis, fundamental limitations in sample size, external validation, and fairness preclude immediate clinical deployment. The framework demonstrates technical innovation but requires substantial additional work---such as multi-center federated learning for privacy-preserving collaboration, GAN-based image synthesis to augment training diversity, attention-based mechanisms for improved cross-modal integration, and population-appropriate calibration methods---before safe clinical integration (El Emam et al., 2020; Shin et al., 2018; Vaswani et al., 2017; Gal et al., 2017). Without addressing these challenges systematically, even sophisticated AI systems risk becoming expensive digital tools that perpetuate rather than solve clinical problems, undermining the promise of AI-enhanced healthcare (Varoquaux, 2018).

\subsubsection{Clinical Translation}

Future work should prioritize multi-center validation studies with diverse patient populations, explicit fairness-aware training protocols, and comprehensive regulatory compliance frameworks. The development of standardized evaluation protocols for rare disease AI models will be crucial for clinical adoption.

\subsubsection{Methodological Enhancements}

Advanced techniques such as federated learning, synthetic data generation, and attention-based multimodal fusion should be explored to address current limitations while maintaining the framework's practical advantages.

